[
    {
        "content": "<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">bar</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">h</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- makes sense, `n` is explicit in `Foo.bar`</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">bar</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- wat</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">bar</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I believe the relevant section of the language reference is <a href=\"https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/latest/Terms/Function-Application/#generalized-field-notation\">generalized field notation</a> but it says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>the term before the dot becomes an argument to the function. Specifically, it becomes the first explicit argument that would not be a type error. Aside from that, the application is elaborated as usual.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>So it looks to me like it says <code>h.bar</code> should still be missing the first parameter.<br>\nI'm confused, is this intended? Is it documented somewhere? What are the rules of it?</p>",
        "id": 590903959,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1777146992
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>And on the topic of dot-notation in the language reference, when describing <a href=\"https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/latest/Terms/Identifiers/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Terms--Identifiers--Leading--___\">leading dot</a> it says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>If the type is not an application of a constant (e.g., a function, a metavariable, or a universe) then it doesn't have a namespace.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>which misses this cool behavior with implications, where the namespace comes from the RHS:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∨</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">inl</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 590903969,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1777147000
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Is this relevant?</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">bar</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 590905153,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael",
        "timestamp": 1777148181
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>No it doesn't skip anything</p>",
        "id": 590905178,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1777148205
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Following this example, a fun thought experiment is: if no such skipping is allowed, what type <code>h.bar</code> should be? <br>\nYou would start writing <code>(n' : Nat) → (?x = ?x)</code>, but then there isn't a good answer what to fill in at <code>?x</code>: it can't be the variable <code>n'</code> because you already specified it to be <code>n</code> by <code>h</code>, but it can't be just <code>n</code> because it needs to be related to <code>n'</code></p>",
        "id": 590906975,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1777149993
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ooh, interesting! Then I guess a good answer would be an error? Similar to when it can't infer universe levels because of missing parameters, or when the values of implicit params can't be inferred?<br>\nThis case is different because there's no missing information, but I think it's a surprising behavior that doesn't match Lean in other cases.<br>\nThings get even stranger if we add more parameters to <code>Foo.bar</code>, then the position of <code>n</code> among them starts to matter similar to params with default values.</p>",
        "id": 590907350,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1777150383
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Indeed if you force it to do this, it is an error</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">bar</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- Application type mismatch</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">trivial</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 590907463,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1777150520
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"933054\">Snir Broshi</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/dot-notation.20skips.20explicit.20parameters/near/590907350\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Things get even stranger if we add more parameters to <code>Foo.bar</code>, then the position of <code>n</code> among them starts to matter similar to params with default values.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Well, in the example, the <code>n</code> parameter can only ever have one value; it has to be the same value that <code>n</code> takes in the type of <code>h</code>, <code>Foo n</code>.</p>\n<p>So the answer to Weiyi's question would appear to be that the no-skipping <code>h.bar</code> has type <code>{n' : Nat // n' = n} → n = n</code>. (Or it could yield <code>n' = n'</code>, I guess; I don't really see why <code>n'</code> needs to be mentioned. They're the same proposition anyway.) <code>{n' : Nat // n' = (n : Nat)}</code> is a singleton type, so a function of that type is a constant function, and the field notation is simplifying it automatically.</p>\n<p>If you give more parameters to <code>Foo.bar</code>, what would be different?</p>",
        "id": 592169700,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael",
        "timestamp": 1777588345
    }
]